$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{x-2}{1-\sqrt{3x-5}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{x-2}{1-\sqrt{3x-5}}\cdot\dfrac{1+\sqrt{3x-5}}{1+\sqrt{3x-5}}\qquad\gray{\text{Rationalize the denominator}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{(x-2)(1+\sqrt{3x-5})}{1^2-(3x-5)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{(x-2)}(1+\sqrt{3x-5})}{-3\cancel{(x-2)}}\qquad\gray{\text{Cancel out common factors}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{1+\sqrt{3x-5}}{-3} \text{, for }x\neq 2
\end{align}$

This means that the two expressions have the same value for all $x$-values (in their domains) except for $2$.

We can now use the following theorem:

If $f(x)=g(x)$ for all $x$-values in a given interval except for $x=c$, then $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to c}f(x)=\lim_{x\to c}g(x)$.
